## 2026-09-12 - real controller activation failure
- Purpose: Diagnose controller_manager rejection of arm/gripper/state broadcaster activation.
- Investigation: Logs show the hardware plugin initializes successfully, then all controllers fail interface claiming; the launch file also attempted parallel spawners, and a later edit exposed an undefined spawner variable.
- Result: Fixed the launch spawner argument and changed startup to serialized timers; build of edgepick_bringup passed. Runtime activation still needs confirmation on the robot.
- Next: Relaunch and verify `ros2 control list_controllers`; if interfaces remain unavailable, capture the first hardware/plugin error before controller spawning.

## 2026-09-12 - I2C trajectory frequency limit
- Purpose: Resolve the real-arm symptom where the first trajectory moved partially and later goals reported success without completing.
- Investigation: The drive board was known to reject or mishandle high-frequency MoveIt updates. The controller was publishing at 100 Hz while the I2C path applied per-cycle commands; the board could not reliably consume that stream.
- Result: Set `controller_manager.update_rate` to 20 Hz, increased the gateway minimum command interval to 50 ms, and added trajectory goal/velocity constraints. Real-arm testing succeeded at 20 Hz, including repeated angle changes.
- Next: Measure the highest stable rate by testing 25, 30, 40, then 50 Hz; stop at the first missed, partial, or delayed move and use the previous rate as the operating limit.

## 2026-09-12 - intermittent I2C faults and hard motion limits
- Purpose: Prevent intermittent real-arm stalls, false action success, and unsafe direct action goals.
- Investigation: The 40 Hz test rate exceeded the previously verified stable range. Each feedback sample synchronously polled six servo registers with up to five retries, so NACKs could block the controller-manager loop. The ros2_control command interfaces also lacked explicit min/max values, while transport limits alone did not guarantee early action rejection.
- Result: Restored the safe 20 Hz default, set a 50 ms command gate, reduced feedback polling to 150 ms with two attempts, kept the component recoverable after transient feedback failures, added ROS-radian limits to each command interface, and reject out-of-range commands in the hardware write path. Added strict trajectory end constraints and disabled partial goals.
- Next: Validate on hardware with an in-range move, an out-of-range goal, repeated commands after an induced I2C fault, and confirm action results are aborted/ rejected rather than successful.

## 2026-09-12 - VLA pre-integration dry-run gate
- Purpose: Prepare VLA integration without allowing unverified model outputs to move the real arm.
- Investigation: `smolvla_node` previously published directly to `/arm_group_controller/joint_trajectory`, bypassing MoveIt and its planning scene; action units/mapping are not yet validated on the real robot.
- Result: Added `execute_trajectory` parameter, default `false`. VLA inference and `/edgepick/vla/action` publication remain available while trajectory execution is disabled by default. Python syntax and builds of `edgepick_interfaces`, `edgepick_vla`, and `edgepick_bringup` passed.
- Next: Validate VLA action units, frame/timestamp freshness, output limits, and a single controlled execution path before enabling `execute_trajectory:=true`.

## 2026-09-13 - VLA real execution wiring
- Purpose: Diagnose why VLA messages changed while the real arm did not move.
- Investigation: The launch parameter `execute_trajectory` remained false, so the node published only `/edgepick/vla/action`. After enabling execution, the arm controller rejected six-joint trajectories because `arm_group_controller` accepts only Arm1_Joint through Arm5_Joint.
- Result: Launch now converts `execute_trajectory` explicitly and defaults the real VLA launch to true; trajectory publication sends only the five arm joints while retaining `grip_joint` in `VLAAction`. Rebuilt `edgepick_bringup` and `edgepick_vla`; real VLA execution subsequently succeeded.
- Next: Keep point-cloud validity as a separate RGB-D issue; it did not block this verified arm execution path.

## 2026-09-16 - VLA safe-route integration in Big
- Purpose: Route model proposals through Safety Gate, MoveIt and the existing ros2_control CommandGateway; keep all project code and the Python environment in Big.
- Investigation/optimization: Removed the old VLA direct-trajectory publisher. Added strict model contract, RGB-D obstacle/target updates, destination recheck, collision-aware payload, watchdogs, fault latch, MuJoCo action backend and detector compression/export/metrics scripts. Cached mesh self-filter bodies after a dynamic-obstacle test exposed seconds of scene-update delay. Real feedback loss now blocks writes immediately and persistent failures abort control.
- Result: Seven ROS packages built; safe-package C++/Python checks and affected hardware/bringup tests passed; 10 isolated ROS Mock checks passed including cancellation during execution. CPU pruning smoke test removed 7.70% of parameters at the tested setting, and distillation gradient / MuJoCo geometry-step checks passed. Big/.venv-vla imported all model dependencies and passed the MuJoCo ROS trajectory/cancellation check. These are not mAP, final size reduction or task success-rate measurements.
- Next: User supplies the final checkpoint; validate model rollout, RGB-D registration and extrinsics before real-hardware commissioning. No physical motion or real grasp was tested in this work.
